{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Usage-log pricing silently drops Anthropic and OpenAI rows: the provider-prefixed model_ref LookupErrors\n\n## THIS IS A REGRESSION INTRODUCED BY THIS EPIC\n\nBefore the provider-seam work, the usage log recorded a BARE model id (`claude-opus-4-8`), which\ngenai-prices resolves, so Anthropic prices displayed correctly. This epic made model strings\nPROVIDER-QUALIFIED, and `agent_call.py` records `model=ran_model` verbatim — so the stored id became\n`anthropic:claude-opus-4-8`, which genai-prices cannot resolve. `ran_model`'s history runs through this\nepic's own tickets: 747e149455 (2932, first-class Bedrock), cc33, e6bd, 8455, 5e75.\n\nSo the correct close class is `regression`, not `preexisting`, and this belongs INSIDE epic 061c rather\nthan beside it.\n\n## Mechanism\n\n`usage_log._price_row` (src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py:412) passes the STORED `provider:model` string as\n`model_ref` to `genai_prices.calc_price`. When that id does not resolve, genai-prices raises\n`LookupError` — its unknown-model signal — which `_price_row` catches at :429 and turns into \"unpriced\".\nThe catch is correct as a policy (pricing must never break `summarize`) but it makes the failure SILENT:\nthe row is dropped from the cost total with no warning, because `LookupError` is swallowed before the\n`WARNING` branch.\n\n## MEASURED against the installed genai-prices, using the same call shape as `_price_row`\n\n    provider_id=\"anthropic\", model_ref=\"anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\"            -> LookupError  (blank)\n    provider_id=\"anthropic\", model_ref=\"claude-sonnet-4-6\"                      -> OK  $0.004500\n    provider_id=\"openai\",    model_ref=\"openai:gpt-5.4\"                         -> LookupError  (blank)\n    provider_id=\"openai\",    model_ref=\"gpt-5.4\"                                -> OK  $0.004000\n    provider_id=\"openai\",    model_ref=\"openai:gpt-5.4-mini\"                    -> LookupError  (blank)\n    provider_id=\"openai\",    model_ref=\"gpt-5.4-mini\"                           -> OK  $0.001200\n    provider_id=\"bedrock\",   model_ref=\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\" -> OK  $0.004950\n    control: provider_id=\"openai\", model_ref=\"gpt-4o\"                           -> OK  $0.003500\n\n## The defect is ASYMMETRIC, and that is what makes it harmful rather than merely untidy\n\nONLY the `bedrock:` prefix form happens to resolve. Anthropic AND OpenAI both read blank. Every model\ninvolved is well known to genai-prices — the bare forms all price. So a provider cost comparison shows\nfigures for Bedrock and nothing for the other two, which reads as \"Bedrock is the expensive arm\" when it\nis in fact the only arm being measured. That is a wrong-direction bias in exactly the comparison this\nepic exists to inform, and it is why the CI provider matrix's cost table\n[rebar:f124-f14a-fd62-4973] had to be computed BY HAND from logged token counts.\n\n## Blast radius is not limited to CI\n\n`summarize` renders the \"est. cost\" column for `rebar usage` output generally\n(src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py:444), so ordinary local and review-bot cost reporting is affected too, not\nonly the external-integration job summary. The observable symptom is the\n`est. cost excludes N unpriced calls` line on runs where genai-prices demonstrably knows every model.\n\n## Approach\n\nPass genai-prices an id it can resolve, keeping `provider_id` as the provider signal it already is.\n\nThe naive fix — strip everything before the first `:` — is WRONG for Bedrock, whose remainder\n(`us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6`) is not genai-prices' own AWS id shape either (its AWS ids look like\n`regional.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6-v1:0`). So this needs a provider-aware translation, and per this\nepic's standing decision it must be decided by REGISTRY MEMBERSHIP rather than prefix sniffing or a\n`split(\":\")` — the attested guard\n`test_capabilities_module_still_has_no_provider_name_prefix_matching` exists for exactly this class of\nshortcut.\n\nDo NOT remove the `LookupError` -> unpriced policy; pricing must stay best-effort and must never break\n`summarize`. The fix is to stop feeding it an unresolvable id, and to make a genuinely-unknown model\nvisibly unpriced rather than indistinguishable from a mis-formatted one.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An Anthropic usage row PRICES rather than silently dropping to unpriced, proven by a test that\n      FAILS before the fix on a realistic stored row (`provider=\"anthropic\"`,\n      `model=\"anthropic:claude-...\"`).\n- [ ] An OpenAI usage row also prices — the fix covers ALL affected providers, not just Anthropic. A fix\n      that handled only the `anthropic:` prefix would leave OpenAI blank and the comparison bias intact.\n- [ ] A Bedrock usage row still prices, and the Anthropic/Bedrock rate relationship is asserted so the\n      asymmetry cannot silently return.\n- [ ] The id translation is decided by REGISTRY MEMBERSHIP, not by prefix matching or `split(\":\")`, and a\n      test pins that — consistent with `capabilities.py`'s attested no-prefix-matching guard.\n- [ ] A model genuinely unknown to genai-prices is still tolerated as unpriced (pricing never breaks\n      `summarize`) and is DISTINGUISHABLE in output from a row dropped because its id was mis-formatted.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass by exit code; the default selection is green; the config-read\n      and env-drift gates pass.\n","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785786191698383001,"uuid":"e317b1f9-5b9d-444e-8181-ba0aaac7cbcf"}